        "original_id": "dbea81b9-f3d4-5610-9d15-f0cf945d25d6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Hill's Tavern, Century Inn, National Road\n- Concepts: historic names, continuous operation, transportation routes\n\nFacts:\n- The National Road was built between 1811 and 1837.\n- The National Road was 620 miles long and connected the Potomac River and the Ohio River.\n- The National Road served as a main transport route to the West for thousands of settlers.\n- In the early 1900s, Hill's Tavern was known for a period as the Central Hotel.\n- Hill's Tavern was later called the Century Inn.\n- The Century Inn had been the oldest tavern in continuous use on the National Road until a fire ended its 221 years of continuous operation.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
